// Day 10 question pool: Domain 3, Design High-Performing Architectures. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(

// ---- Task 3.1: high-performing and scalable storage ----

{id:"q10-001", day:10, dom:3, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"A media analytics company stores millions of small JSON event files in one S3 bucket, all under the same prefix, <code>events/</code>. A fleet of EC2 instances in the same Region reads and writes these objects in parallel. During peak hours the application receives HTTP 503 Slow Down responses, even though the total data volume is modest. The company wants the bucket to sustain a much higher request rate without changing Region or storage class. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Spread the objects across many prefixes so requests are distributed among partitions","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use the accelerate endpoint","Upload each object with multipart upload so that the writes run in parallel","Enable S3 Versioning so concurrent writes to the same key do not conflict"],
 a:[0],
 x:["S3 request-rate limits apply per partitioned prefix (at least 3,500 writes and 5,500 reads per second each), so spreading keys across more prefixes multiplies the rate the bucket can sustain.","Transfer Acceleration speeds long-distance transfers through edge locations; it does not raise per-prefix request limits, and the instances are already in the same Region.","Multipart upload helps with large objects; for small JSON files it only adds requests and does nothing for the per-prefix request rate.","Versioning keeps prior versions of objects for recovery; it does not increase request throughput and is not a fix for 503 Slow Down errors."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q10-002", day:10, dom:3, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"A video-sharing startup collects raw footage from creators on several continents into a single S3 bucket in us-east-1. Files are typically 3–8 GB. Creators report slow uploads, and when a connection drops near the end, the entire file must be sent again. The footage must be written directly to that bucket. Which combination of actions will improve upload speed and reliability? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Have the upload client use parallel byte-range fetches","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration and upload through its endpoint","Create an S3 gateway VPC endpoint for the bucket","Use multipart upload so parts are sent in parallel and retried individually","Upload to buckets near the creators and replicate them to the central bucket"],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Byte-range fetches are ranged GET requests that speed up downloads; they do nothing for uploads.","Transfer Acceleration routes uploads to the nearest edge location and then over the AWS backbone to the bucket, which speeds long-distance transfers.","A gateway endpoint gives resources inside a VPC private access to S3; creators upload from the internet, so it does not apply.","Multipart upload sends parts in parallel for higher throughput, and a failed part can be retried alone instead of restarting the whole multi-gigabyte file.","Cross-Region Replication copies objects after they are written elsewhere, so the footage would not be written directly to the central bucket and would arrive there later, not sooner."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q10-003", day:10, dom:3, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"A genomics research team stores sequencing files of 50–200 GB each in S3. An analysis tool on EC2 instances in the same Region downloads each file over a single stream before processing, and the downloads take too long. The tool can process any segment of a file independently. Which approach will MOST improve download throughput?",
 o:["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration and download through the accelerate endpoint","Re-upload the files with multipart upload so later downloads are faster","Issue parallel byte-range GET requests for different segments over multiple connections","Move the files to S3 Standard-IA to raise the retrieval throughput"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Transfer Acceleration helps distant clients reach a bucket over the internet through edge locations; instances in the same Region gain no throughput from it, and one stream stays one stream.","Multipart upload speeds up writes; a single-stream download of the object is just as slow afterward unless the client parallelizes its GET requests.","Byte-range fetches let the client download many segments of an object at once over parallel connections, aggregating far more throughput than one stream.","Standard-IA has the same performance as S3 Standard but adds a per-GB retrieval fee; storage class does not change download throughput."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q10-004", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EBS & instance store","EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A database runs on an EC2 instance with an io2 Block Express volume, created empty, that is provisioned for 60,000 IOPS. Monitoring shows the volume never delivers more than about 20,000 IOPS, and I/O latency rises at that point even though the database needs more. The instance's CPU and memory are underused. What should a solutions architect do to get the provisioned performance?",
 o:["Increase the volume's provisioned IOPS to 100,000 so that it has more headroom above the demand",
    "Move to an instance type whose EBS bandwidth and IOPS limits exceed the volume's provisioned levels",
    "Change the volume to General Purpose SSD (gp3) and provision IOPS and throughput independently",
    "Enable fast snapshot restore for the volume so that all of its blocks are initialized in advance"],
 a:[1],
 x:["The volume already offers three times the IOPS being delivered, so the bottleneck is elsewhere and more provisioned IOPS only adds cost.",
    "Every instance type has a maximum EBS bandwidth and IOPS; when that limit is lower than the volume's provisioned performance, the instance caps it, so a type with higher EBS limits is needed.",
    "Changing the volume type does not remove the instance-level limit that is capping performance, and gp3 gives less consistent latency than io2 Block Express for this database.",
    "The volume was created empty, not from a snapshot, so there are no uninitialized blocks; fast snapshot restore would change nothing."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q10-005", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs PostgreSQL on an EC2 instance with a 300 GiB gp2 data volume. Monitoring shows the volume pinned at its IOPS limit during business hours, while only half of its capacity is used. The company wants more IOPS and throughput without paying for storage it does not need and without taking the database offline. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Grow the gp2 volume to 4 TiB so its baseline IOPS scales with the size","Change the volume type to Cold HDD (sc1) for higher sequential throughput","Restore a snapshot to a new gp2 volume with fast snapshot restore enabled","Modify the volume to gp3 and provision IOPS and throughput independently of size"],
 a:[3],
 x:["gp2 IOPS are tied to size (3 IOPS per GiB), so this works only by paying for terabytes of storage the company does not need.","sc1 is the lowest-performance HDD type, meant for infrequently accessed data; it would make database I/O far worse.","Fast snapshot restore removes first-read latency on restored volumes, but the new gp2 volume has the same size-based IOPS limit and the swap needs downtime.","Elastic Volumes can change gp2 to gp3 while the volume stays in use, and gp3 lets you provision IOPS and throughput separately from capacity."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q10-006", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EBS & instance store","EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A gaming analytics company runs a self-managed distributed NoSQL database on EC2. The database replicates every write to three nodes in different Availability Zones, and if a node is lost, the cluster automatically rebuilds its data from the other replicas. Each node needs the highest possible random I/O performance and the lowest latency for its local data files. Which storage option best meets these requirements?",
 o:["Storage optimized instances with local NVMe instance store volumes","Memory optimized instances with io2 Block Express EBS volumes","A shared Amazon EFS file system in the Max I/O performance mode","An S3 Express One Zone directory bucket mounted on each node"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Instance store NVMe disks are physically attached to the host and deliver the highest IOPS and lowest latency; losing a node's data is acceptable because the cluster re-replicates it.","io2 Block Express is fast and durable, but it is network-attached, so its latency is higher than local NVMe, and its durability is redundant because the application already replicates.","EFS is a network file system with higher per-operation latency, and Max I/O mode trades even more latency for parallelism; it is not suited to database data files.","S3 Express One Zone is object storage; it does not provide the block-level random I/O a database engine needs for its data files."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3c1","d1p3"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q10-007", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EFS"],
 q:"A marketing agency keeps 200 GB of design assets on Amazon EFS in the Bursting throughput mode. A rendering fleet reads the files in unpredictable bursts that need very high throughput for short periods, then sits idle for hours. Lately, throughput collapses partway through render jobs. The agency wants throughput to scale automatically with the workload and to pay only for the throughput it uses. Which change should a solutions architect make?",
 o:["Switch the file system to Provisioned throughput sized for the peak","Re-create the file system using the Max I/O performance mode","Switch the file system to the Elastic throughput mode","Move the data to a new EFS One Zone file system"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Provisioned throughput removes the burst-credit limit but bills for the peak level around the clock, even while the fleet is idle for hours.","Max I/O is a performance mode that allows more parallel operations at higher latency; it does not lift the throughput limit set by depleted burst credits.","Elastic throughput scales up and down automatically with the workload and charges for the throughput actually used, which fits spiky, unpredictable access.","A One Zone file system lowers cost by keeping data in a single AZ; it does not add throughput."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3p3","d3w1"], doc:"efs"},

{id:"q10-008", day:10, dom:3, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle","S3 features"],
 q:"A machine learning team trains models on EC2 GPU instances in one Availability Zone. Each training epoch reads millions of small files from Amazon S3, and request latency to S3 Standard has become the bottleneck that leaves the GPUs idle. The training dataset is a copy that can be re-created from the source bucket at any time. The team needs consistent single-digit millisecond request latency from object storage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and read the training files through the accelerate endpoint",
    "Transition the training files to S3 Intelligent-Tiering so that frequently read files stay in a fast tier",
    "Put a CloudFront distribution in front of the bucket and read the training files through the distribution",
    "Copy the dataset to an S3 Express One Zone directory bucket in the same Availability Zone as the instances"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Transfer Acceleration routes long-distance transfers over the AWS edge network; it does not lower request latency for instances in the same Region.",
    "Intelligent-Tiering optimizes storage cost by access pattern, and its frequent access tier performs like S3 Standard.",
    "CloudFront caches content for internet viewers; for EC2 instances in the same Region it adds an extra hop and is not designed for this access pattern.",
    "S3 Express One Zone stores data in a single AZ and delivers consistent single-digit millisecond latency, much lower than S3 Standard; colocating it with the GPUs suits training data that can be re-created."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3w1"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q10-009", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"A logistics company runs a nightly log-processing job on EC2 that reads and writes multi-terabyte files sequentially in large blocks. The job is limited by throughput, not by IOPS, and the data is not on the boot volume. The company wants the lowest-cost EBS volume type that still delivers high sustained throughput for this access pattern. Which volume type should be used?",
 o:["Cold HDD (sc1)","Throughput Optimized HDD (st1)","Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2 Block Express)","General Purpose SSD (gp3) with maximum throughput"],
 a:[1],
 x:["sc1 is cheaper still, but its throughput is much lower; it is meant for colder data that is scanned infrequently, not a nightly throughput-bound job.","st1 is a low-cost HDD volume designed for frequently accessed, large sequential workloads such as log processing and big data, with high sustained throughput.","io2 is priced for high, sustained IOPS and low latency, which this sequential, throughput-bound job does not need.","gp3 can deliver the throughput, but SSD storage costs more per GB than st1 for multi-terabyte data, so it is not the lowest-cost fit."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q10-010", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EFS","Containers"],
 q:"A healthcare software vendor runs a document-processing service as Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate across three Availability Zones. Every task must read and write the same set of files concurrently using standard Linux file operations, and the storage must grow and shrink automatically as documents are added and removed. Which storage solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["An Amazon EFS file system mounted through the task definition","An EBS io2 volume with Multi-Attach shared by all of the tasks","An Amazon FSx for Lustre scratch file system shared by the tasks","An S3 bucket mounted in each container with Mountpoint for Amazon S3"],
 a:[0],
 x:["EFS is a regional, elastic NFS file system that Fargate tasks can mount natively; it grows and shrinks automatically and serves concurrent readers and writers in all AZs.","Multi-Attach works only for EC2 instances in a single AZ and needs a cluster-aware file system; it cannot be shared by Fargate tasks across three AZs.","Amazon ECS has no FSx for Lustre volume type that Fargate tasks can mount, and a scratch file system has fixed capacity that does not grow on its own and keeps no replicas.","Mountpoint for S3 does not provide full file-system semantics, such as editing existing files in place or file locking, so concurrent read-write file workloads do not fit."],
 ref:["d10e2","d3p3","d1w3"], doc:"efs"},

// ---- Task 3.2: high-performing and elastic compute ----

{id:"q10-011", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A research institute runs a tightly coupled computational fluid dynamics simulation that uses MPI across 64 EC2 instances. Nodes exchange messages constantly, and the job's run time is dominated by inter-node latency. The application supports OS-bypass networking through libfabric. The institute wants the lowest possible latency and highest throughput between instances. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Launch the instances in a spread placement group","Launch the instances in a cluster placement group in one Availability Zone","Attach an Elastic Fabric Adapter to each instance on a supported instance type","Distribute the instances evenly across three Availability Zones","Launch the instances in a partition placement group across multiple Availability Zones"],
 a:[1,2],
 x:["A spread placement group puts each instance on distinct hardware for fault isolation and allows only seven running instances per AZ, so it cannot pack 64 nodes close together.","A cluster placement group packs instances close together inside one AZ, giving the lowest latency and highest per-flow throughput between them.","EFA adds OS-bypass networking for MPI and similar libraries, cutting inter-node latency well below what standard TCP networking provides.","Spreading nodes over AZs improves resilience but adds inter-AZ latency, the exact bottleneck this job suffers from.","Partition placement groups isolate groups of instances on separate racks for large distributed systems such as HDFS; they are about failure isolation, not minimum latency."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w2"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q10-012", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EC2 instance types","High availability design"],
 q:"A data platform team is deploying a 90-node Hadoop HDFS cluster on EC2 in one Availability Zone. HDFS places block replicas on different racks, so the team needs to know which nodes share underlying hardware and to make sure a single rack failure affects only a subset of the nodes. Which EC2 feature meets these requirements?",
 o:["A cluster placement group","A spread placement group","A partition placement group","Dedicated Hosts with host affinity"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A cluster placement group packs instances close together for low latency, so one hardware failure could take out many nodes, and it exposes no rack topology.","A spread placement group is limited to seven running instances per AZ, far too few for a 90-node cluster.","A partition placement group places each partition on separate racks and exposes the partition number to instances, so HDFS can place replicas in different failure domains.","Dedicated Hosts address licensing and compliance needs; host affinity pins instances to a host but does not provide rack-aware partitions for failure isolation."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w2"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q10-013", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A fintech startup runs stateless microservices written in Go and Python as containers on EC2 instances from the M6i (x86) family. Its build pipeline already publishes multi-architecture container images for x86 and Arm. Leadership asks for the best price performance for these general-purpose workloads with minimal application changes. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Move to compute optimized C6i instances one size larger","Run the same instance types as Dedicated Instances","Move to burstable T3 instances with unlimited mode enabled","Move to Graviton-based general purpose instances such as M8g"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A larger x86 compute optimized instance costs more and gives up memory per vCPU; it raises cost rather than improving price performance.","Dedicated Instances provide single-tenant hardware for compliance; they cost more and deliver no extra performance.","Burstable instances suit low average CPU use; steady microservice load would accrue surplus credit charges in unlimited mode.","AWS Graviton (Arm) instances deliver better price performance than comparable x86 instances, and the existing multi-architecture images run on them without code changes."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1p3","d1w2"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q10-014", day:10, dom:3, tags:["EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A retail company runs an in-memory analytics engine on c6i.4xlarge instances. Under load, the engine starts paging to disk and queries slow down sharply, while CPU utilization stays around 25%. The vendor confirms that performance depends on keeping the entire working set in RAM. Which change will MOST improve performance?",
 o:["Move to memory optimized R-family instances with similar vCPUs and much more RAM","Move to larger compute optimized C-family instances to gain more memory","Move to storage optimized I-family instances so paging hits local NVMe disks","Place the existing instances in a cluster placement group"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Memory optimized instances offer far more RAM per vCPU, so the working set fits in memory without paying for CPU the engine does not use.","Compute optimized instances have the least memory per vCPU; getting enough RAM this way means paying for many idle vCPUs.","Faster paging to local NVMe is still far slower than RAM; it treats the symptom instead of the memory shortage.","A cluster placement group lowers network latency between instances; it does not add memory."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1p3","d1w2"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q10-015", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Step Functions","Lambda"],
 q:"Every night about 2 million images land under an S3 prefix. Today a single Lambda function lists the objects and processes them one after another, and it reaches the 15-minute timeout long before it finishes. The company wants to process each image with an existing Lambda function at high parallelism, retry failed items, record which items failed, and run a summary step only after all images are done, with minimal orchestration code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Raise the existing function's memory to the maximum so that it finishes all images within 15 minutes",
    "Configure S3 Event Notifications so that each new image invokes the processing function on its own",
    "Use a Step Functions Distributed Map state over the S3 prefix that invokes the function for each image",
    "Run a multithreaded script on a large EC2 instance that downloads and processes all images in a loop"],
 a:[2],
 x:["More memory makes each invocation faster, but processing 2 million images one after another cannot fit into a single 15-minute invocation.",
    "Per-object events give parallelism, but nothing tracks when all images are finished to trigger the summary step or collects the failures.",
    "A Distributed Map reads the items from S3, runs child workflows at high concurrency with retries and failure thresholds, can write results to S3, and moves on to the summary step after every item completes.",
    "A single instance is a server to manage, has limited parallelism, and needs custom code for retries and failure tracking."],
 ref:["d10e3","d9e2"], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q10-016", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A company runs a Java 21 Lambda function behind Amazon API Gateway. Traffic is sporadic, and each time a new execution environment starts, framework initialization takes several seconds, so some users see slow responses. The company wants to cut this startup latency substantially without paying to keep execution environments initialized all the time. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Turn on Lambda SnapStart for the function",
    "Configure provisioned concurrency on an alias",
    "Increase the function's timeout to 15 minutes",
    "Set reserved concurrency on the function",
    "Invoke a published version through an alias"],
 a:[0,4],
 x:["SnapStart takes a snapshot of the initialized execution environment when a version is published and resumes new environments from it, cutting Java startup latency without paying for idle environments.",
    "Provisioned concurrency keeps environments initialized, but the company pays for them continuously, which it wants to avoid, and it cannot be combined with SnapStart.",
    "The timeout limits how long an invocation may run; it does not make initialization faster.",
    "Reserved concurrency guarantees and caps the function's concurrency; it does not pre-initialize environments or shorten startup.",
    "SnapStart applies only to published function versions and aliases that point to them; invocations of $LATEST do not use the snapshot, so API Gateway must call a version or an alias."],
 ref:["d10e3","d10b2","d1w3"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q10-017", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"An image-processing Lambda function resizes photos uploaded to S3. It is configured with 512 MB of memory, uses about 180 MB, and takes around 40 seconds per image because the resizing is CPU-intensive. The team wants each invocation to finish faster without changing the code. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Increase the function's timeout setting","Increase the function's memory setting","Configure provisioned concurrency for the function","Configure reserved concurrency for the function"],
 a:[1],
 x:["A longer timeout only lets the function run longer before it is stopped; each invocation stays just as slow.","Lambda allocates CPU in proportion to configured memory, so raising memory gives a CPU-bound function more compute and shortens its duration even if it does not need the RAM.","Provisioned concurrency removes cold-start initialization delay; it does not make the handler's CPU work run faster.","Reserved concurrency guarantees and caps how many instances of the function can run at once; it does not change per-invocation speed."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w3","d9b1"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q10-018", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A travel booking company exposes a Lambda-backed API through Amazon API Gateway. The function loads large libraries and creates SDK clients during initialization, so requests that land on new execution environments take several seconds. Responses are unique to each user. The company requires consistently low latency during business hours, when traffic is steady and predictable. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Provisioned concurrency on an alias, scheduled with Application Auto Scaling","Reserved concurrency set to the expected business-hours peak","Raise the function timeout to its 15-minute maximum value","API Gateway caching on all methods with a long TTL"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Provisioned concurrency keeps the requested number of environments initialized ahead of time, removing cold starts; scheduling it covers business hours without paying for it overnight.","Reserved concurrency sets aside capacity but does not pre-initialize environments, so cold starts still occur as the function scales.","The timeout limits how long an invocation may run; it has no effect on initialization latency.","Responses are unique per user, so cached responses would rarely be reused, and requests that miss the cache still hit cold execution environments."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w3","d10b2"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q10-019", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A company runs dozens of Lambda functions in one account. During a marketing campaign, a batch-import function scaled out and consumed most of the account's concurrency, so the payment-authorization function was throttled. The payment function must always be able to scale to 300 concurrent executions, its measured peak. Occasional cold starts are acceptable, and the company does not want to pay for idle pre-initialized environments. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Increase the payment function's memory so each request completes faster","Place an SQS queue in front of the payment function to buffer requests","Configure provisioned concurrency of 300 on the payment function","Configure reserved concurrency of 300 on the payment function"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Faster invocations free capacity sooner but give no guarantee; the batch function could still take the whole concurrency pool.","Payment authorization is a synchronous request, and a queue only delays the work; it does not set aside concurrency for the function.","Provisioned concurrency would also secure capacity, but it bills for initialized environments even when they are idle, which the company wants to avoid.","Reserved concurrency sets aside 300 executions that no other function can use, at no extra charge; it also caps the function at 300, which matches its peak, and normal cold starts still occur."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w3"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q10-020", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A startup is containerizing several REST services to run on Amazon ECS. The team has no operations staff and does not want to patch hosts, choose instance types, or manage cluster capacity. Traffic varies widely during the day, and each service must scale its tasks independently. Which approach meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Amazon EKS with self-managed node groups and the Cluster Autoscaler","AWS Elastic Beanstalk with the Docker platform on EC2 instances","ECS with the Fargate launch type and ECS Service Auto Scaling","ECS with the EC2 launch type and an Auto Scaling group capacity provider"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Self-managed nodes mean managing EC2 instances, AMIs, and upgrades, plus Kubernetes itself; this is the most operational work of the options.","Elastic Beanstalk still provisions EC2 instances whose types and capacity the team must choose, and it does not offer ECS's independent per-service task scaling.","Fargate runs each task on AWS-managed compute, so there are no hosts to patch or size, and Service Auto Scaling scales each service's task count on its own metrics.","The EC2 launch type leaves the team responsible for the container instances' AMIs, patching, and instance-type choices, even with a capacity provider."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1c8","d1w3"], doc:"fargate"},

{id:"q10-021", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Containers","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A pharmaceutical company must run thousands of containerized molecular-docking jobs every night. Each job runs for 1–3 hours, some jobs can start only after others finish, and the company wants to use Spot capacity wherever possible. The team does not want to build or operate its own job scheduler or compute cluster. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["AWS Batch with managed compute environments and job queues","AWS Lambda functions invoked from an Amazon SQS queue","Amazon EMR clusters using instance fleets","An EC2 Auto Scaling group running a custom cron-based job runner"],
 a:[0],
 x:["AWS Batch queues and schedules containerized jobs, supports job dependencies, and provisions and scales On-Demand or Spot capacity in managed compute environments.","A Lambda invocation can run for at most 15 minutes (90 minutes for queue-driven invocations on Lambda Managed Instances), which cannot cover jobs of up to 3 hours, and Lambda has no job dependencies.","EMR is designed for big data frameworks such as Spark and Hadoop, not for scheduling generic containerized jobs with dependencies.","A custom cron runner is exactly the scheduler the team does not want to build and operate."],
 ref:["d10e3","d1w3","d1c7"]},

// ---- Task 3.3: high-performing databases ----

{id:"q10-022", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Aurora","Caching"],
 q:"An e-commerce company runs its product catalog on an Aurora MySQL cluster with a single writer instance. During sales events, the writer's CPU reaches 95% because of repeated read queries for the same popular products, and checkout writes slow down. Which combination of actions will reduce the read load on the writer? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Switch the cluster's storage configuration to Aurora I/O-Optimized","Add Aurora Replicas and send read queries to the cluster's reader endpoint","Turn on the Advanced mode of CloudWatch Database Insights","Route the application's connections through Amazon RDS Proxy","Cache product lookups in Amazon ElastiCache using a cache-aside pattern"],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["I/O-Optimized changes how storage I/O is priced; it does not take repeated read queries off the writer's CPU.","Aurora Replicas share the cluster volume and serve reads; the reader endpoint load-balances read connections across them, offloading the writer.","Database Insights (the DB load analysis formerly labeled Performance Insights) shows which waits and SQL statements make up the load, but it does not reduce that load.","RDS Proxy pools and reuses connections; without replicas every query still executes on the writer, and it does not cache query results.","Serving popular product data from an in-memory cache answers repeated reads in microseconds without touching the database."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2","d3w2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q10-023", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"A SaaS company is launching a new multi-tenant application on Aurora PostgreSQL. Load is hard to predict: it is near idle overnight but can jump tenfold within minutes when a large tenant runs month-end reports. The company wants compute capacity to adjust automatically in fine-grained increments within seconds, without failovers or dropped connections, and without moving off PostgreSQL. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["A provisioned Aurora cluster with a scheduled function that changes its instance class","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with storage autoscaling enabled","Aurora serverless instances in the Aurora PostgreSQL cluster","Amazon DynamoDB tables in on-demand capacity mode"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Changing an instance class requires a reboot or failover, and a schedule cannot follow unpredictable spikes.","Storage autoscaling grows disk space only; it does nothing for compute capacity.","Aurora serverless (formerly Aurora Serverless v2) scales compute in increments as small as 0.5 ACU within seconds while connections stay open, matching unpredictable load and scaling back down when idle.","DynamoDB scales seamlessly, but it is a NoSQL database, so the application would have to be redesigned away from PostgreSQL."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3w2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q10-024", day:10, dom:3, tags:["RDS","Lambda"],
 q:"A ticketing company's API runs on Lambda and writes to an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. During ticket releases, thousands of concurrent invocations each open a new database connection; the database hits its maximum connection limit and spends CPU on connection churn. The company wants to fix this with minimal code changes and without reducing the API's concurrency. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Set reserved concurrency on the function to cap open connections","Add RDS read replicas and spread the connections across them","Add an ElastiCache for Memcached cluster in front of the database","Place Amazon RDS Proxy between the functions and the database"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Capping concurrency would protect the database but directly violates the requirement not to reduce the API's concurrency.","Read replicas accept only reads; the writes and their connections still all go to the primary instance.","A cache can serve repeated reads, but these requests are writes, and each invocation would still open its own database connection.","RDS Proxy keeps a warm pool of database connections and multiplexes many function connections onto it; the functions only change their endpoint."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q10-025", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"An Aurora MySQL cluster has a writer and four Aurora Replicas. Two replicas are small instances that serve the web application's short read queries, and two are much larger instances that were added for heavy reporting queries. All applications use the cluster's reader endpoint, so long reports sometimes land on the small replicas and slow down the web application. Which solution routes the reports to the right replicas with the least effort?",
 o:["Point the reporting tool at the writer instance's endpoint so that reports never run on the small replicas",
    "Create a custom endpoint that contains the two large replicas, and point the reporting tool at that endpoint",
    "Enable Aurora Auto Scaling so that more replicas are added automatically whenever reporting load increases",
    "Give each application the instance endpoints of its replicas and balance connections in application code"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Running reports on the writer moves heavy load onto the instance that handles every write, which hurts the web application even more.",
    "An Aurora custom endpoint load-balances connections across a chosen set of instances, so reports run only on the large replicas and stop slowing down the small ones.",
    "New replicas would simply join the reader endpoint, so reports could still land on the small replicas.",
    "Instance endpoints work, but they push load balancing and failover handling into application code, which is more effort and more fragile."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3w2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q10-026", day:10, dom:3, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A food-delivery platform stores orders in a production DynamoDB table with customerId as the partition key and orderId as the sort key. A new operations dashboard must quickly list every order for a given restaurant within a time range. The table holds hundreds of millions of items, and the dashboard's queries must not read the whole table. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a local secondary index that uses restaurantId as the sort key","Run a Scan with a filter expression on restaurantId and order time","Create a global secondary index keyed on restaurantId and order time","Enable DynamoDB Streams and read the stream for each restaurant"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A local secondary index keeps the base table's partition key (customerId) and can only be created with the table, so it cannot serve restaurant queries on this existing table.","A Scan reads every item and filters afterward, consuming huge read capacity and taking far too long for a dashboard.","A GSI with restaurantId as the partition key and order time as the sort key supports an efficient Query for one restaurant over a time range, and it can be added to an existing table.","Streams expose a 24-hour log of item changes for event processing; they are not a queryable index of all orders."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3p2","d3b3"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q10-027", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"A media company is launching a live quiz app whose traffic varies widely and unpredictably, from almost nothing between shows to very high request rates during live shows that are scheduled at irregular times. The app needs a Valkey-compatible cache in front of its Aurora database. The team does not want to choose node types, size shards, or plan cache capacity. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey",
    "An ElastiCache for Valkey cluster sized for peak",
    "DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of Aurora",
    "ElastiCache for Memcached with auto discovery"],
 a:[0],
 x:["ElastiCache Serverless scales compute, memory, and network capacity automatically with demand, with no node types, shards, or capacity planning, and it is compatible with Valkey.",
    "A node-based cluster sized for the peak requires choosing node types and shard counts, and the company pays for idle peak capacity between shows.",
    "DAX caches only DynamoDB API calls; it cannot sit in front of an Aurora database or serve Valkey commands.",
    "Memcached is not compatible with Valkey commands, and a node-based Memcached cluster still needs node sizing and manual scaling."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q10-028", day:10, dom:3, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A startup is adding a voting feature for live TV events to its app. The DynamoDB table sits idle for days, then receives sudden bursts of traffic during events that the team cannot forecast in size or timing. The team does not want to plan or manage capacity, and it wants to pay only for the reads and writes it actually serves. Which configuration best meets these requirements?",
 o:["Provisioned capacity with DynamoDB auto scaling","On-demand capacity mode","Provisioned capacity with reserved capacity purchased","Provisioned capacity with a DAX cluster in front"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Auto scaling adjusts provisioned capacity only after utilization changes, so sudden bursts can be throttled, and the team still pays for the minimum capacity while the table is idle.","On-demand mode needs no capacity planning, instantly accommodates traffic up to double its previous peak, and bills per request, so idle days cost nothing for throughput.","Reserved capacity is a commitment to a steady baseline; it suits predictable traffic and is charged even when the table is idle.","DAX accelerates reads only; the voting writes would still need capacity planning on the table."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3b3"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q10-029", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"A gaming company needs a real-time global leaderboard that ranks millions of players by score and returns the top 100 players, or any player's current rank, in under a millisecond. The data store must replicate across Availability Zones and fail over automatically so the leaderboard survives a node failure. Which solution best meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached with several nodes","Amazon DynamoDB with DAX and a GSI on the score attribute","Amazon RDS for MySQL with a read replica and ORDER BY queries","Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey using sorted sets, with Multi-AZ"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Memcached is a simple key-value cache with no sorted sets for ranking, and node-based Memcached clusters do not replicate data or fail over.","DynamoDB cannot compute a player's rank natively; ranking would need large queries over the index, and DAX only caches item and query results.","Sorting millions of rows on disk-based storage for each request cannot meet sub-millisecond latency.","Valkey (like Redis OSS) sorted sets keep members ordered by score, returning top-N and rank in sub-millisecond time, and Multi-AZ replication provides automatic failover."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2","d3c4"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q10-030", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"A news site caches article data from its Amazon RDS database in ElastiCache using lazy loading with a 24-hour TTL. Editors complain that corrections can take up to a day to appear, and the business now requires edits to show immediately. The team wants to keep the cache shielding the database and to avoid filling the cache with articles nobody reads. Which caching approach best meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add write-through updates on each article save, keeping lazy loading and a TTL","Keep lazy loading as it is but reduce the TTL to 60 seconds","Replace lazy loading with a nightly job that loads every article into the cache","Remove the cache and serve all reads from RDS read replicas"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Writing to the cache whenever an article is saved refreshes the cached copy immediately, while lazy loading and the TTL keep only requested articles in the cache.","A shorter TTL still serves stale data for up to a minute and causes many more cache misses that hit the database.","Pre-loading fills the cache with articles nobody reads, and edits made during the day stay stale until the next run.","Dropping the cache gives up the database protection the team wants to keep, and replicas can still lag behind the primary."],
 ref:["d10e4","d10c2"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q10-031", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"A social networking startup wants to add 'people you may know' suggestions and fraud-ring detection. Queries must traverse relationships several hops deep, for example friends of friends who share a device or payment card, and return results in milliseconds across billions of connections. The team wants a fully managed database built for this access pattern. Which database should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility)","Amazon Neptune","Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra)","Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with recursive SQL queries"],
 a:[1],
 x:["DocumentDB stores JSON documents; following multi-hop relationships would require many lookups or joins in the application.","Neptune is a managed graph database that stores relationships natively and traverses them quickly with Gremlin, openCypher, or SPARQL.","Keyspaces is a wide-column store optimized for key-based access at scale; it has no way to traverse relationships across hops.","Recursive joins work for shallow hierarchies but slow down sharply as hop depth and data volume grow."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3w2"]},

{id:"q10-032", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"An online marketplace keeps its product catalog in DynamoDB. Shoppers need a search box that tolerates typos, ranks results by relevance, and shows faceted filters such as brand and price range, with results in well under a second across millions of products. The company wants a managed service that stays in sync with the catalog without custom pipelines. Which solution should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["A DynamoDB global secondary index on product name queried with begins_with","An Aurora MySQL replica of the catalog queried with LIKE '%term%' filters","Amazon OpenSearch Service, kept in sync from DynamoDB through a zero-ETL integration","Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra) with a table keyed on product name"],
 a:[2],
 x:["begins_with matches only exact prefixes; it cannot handle typos, relevance ranking, or facets.","Leading-wildcard LIKE queries cannot use indexes and scan the table, and they offer no typo tolerance or relevance ranking.","OpenSearch Service provides full-text search with fuzzy matching, relevance scoring, and aggregations for facets, and the DynamoDB zero-ETL integration keeps the index updated.","Keyspaces supports key-based lookups, not full-text relevance search or faceting."],
 ref:["d10e4","d3w2"]},

// ---- Task 3.4: high-performing and scalable networks ----

{id:"q10-033", day:10, dom:3, tags:["CloudFront"],
 q:"A media company serves images and video segments worldwide through an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of a custom origin in one AWS Region. The origin servers are overloaded, and CloudFront reports a low cache hit ratio. The cache key currently includes all viewer headers, cookies, and query strings, although the content does not vary by any of them. Which combination of actions will reduce the number of requests that reach the origin? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the origin servers","Set the distribution's minimum and default TTLs to 0 seconds","Use a cache policy that removes unused headers, cookies, and query strings from the cache key","Move the origin servers to larger instance types","Enable CloudFront Origin Shield in the Region closest to the origin"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Global Accelerator speeds up the network path but does not cache content, so every request would still reach the origin.","Zero TTLs let objects expire immediately unless the origin sends caching headers, sending more requests to the origin, not fewer.","With fewer values in the cache key, identical objects share one cache entry instead of thousands of variants, so far more requests are served from the edge.","Bigger servers add capacity to absorb the load but do not reduce the number of requests reaching the origin.","Origin Shield adds a central caching layer in front of the origin, so cache misses from all edge locations and regional caches are consolidated before they reach it."],
 ref:["d10e5","d1w4","d10b1"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q10-034", day:10, dom:3, tags:["CloudFront"],
 q:"A retail website is served through Amazon CloudFront. On every viewer request, the company needs to normalize the Accept-Language header to a few supported values to improve the cache hit ratio, and to redirect legacy URLs to new paths. The logic is simple, must add minimal latency at very high request volumes, and should be as inexpensive as possible. It needs no network calls and no access to the request body. Which solution should a solutions architect use?",
 o:["Lambda@Edge functions on the origin request event","An AWS WAF web ACL with custom rules on the distribution","CloudFront Functions on the viewer request event","Listener rules on the Application Load Balancer at the origin"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Lambda@Edge suits heavier logic that needs network access or longer run times, at higher cost and latency; origin request triggers run only on cache misses, after the cache key is already chosen.","AWS WAF inspects requests to allow, block, or count them; it is not a tool for rewriting headers to normalize the cache key.","CloudFront Functions run lightweight JavaScript at every edge location in under a millisecond, at a fraction of Lambda@Edge's cost, and are made for header normalization and redirects on viewer requests.","ALB rules act at the origin after the edge cache, so they cannot improve the cache hit ratio, and every redirect would travel all the way to the origin."],
 ref:["d10e5","d10b1"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q10-035", day:10, dom:3, tags:["CloudFront"],
 q:"A company hosts a single-page web application whose static assets are in a private S3 bucket and whose API runs on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. Users around the world report slow page loads. The company wants one domain name, edge caching of static assets, and faster delivery of the uncacheable API over the AWS global network, while keeping the bucket private. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Two Global Accelerator accelerators, one with the S3 bucket and one with the ALB as its endpoint","One CloudFront distribution with the bucket as default origin (via OAC) and an <code>/api/*</code> behavior to the ALB","S3 static website hosting with public read access, plus Route 53 latency-based routing for the ALB","S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket for the assets, plus copies of the ALB stack in more Regions"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Global Accelerator does not cache content and cannot use an S3 bucket as an endpoint.","CloudFront caches the assets at edge locations, origin access control keeps the bucket private, and the API cache behavior proxies calls to the ALB over the AWS network with caching disabled, all under one domain.","Website hosting requires a public bucket, which violates the requirement, and nothing would cache the assets at the edge.","Transfer Acceleration speeds transfers to and from the bucket but does not cache assets for viewers, and extra Regions add cost without providing one domain."],
 ref:["d10e5","d10b1","d10c1"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q10-036", day:10, dom:3, tags:["API Gateway"],
 q:"A company exposes an Amazon API Gateway REST API in eu-west-1 with the default edge-optimized endpoint type. Almost all callers are backend services running on EC2 instances in eu-west-1, plus a few partners on the internet who also call it from Europe. Each request is a unique write, so responses cannot be cached. Latency is higher than the team expects for callers in the same Region. Which change will reduce latency the MOST for these callers?",
 o:["Enable API Gateway caching on the stage with a short TTL for all methods",
    "Change the API's endpoint type from edge-optimized to Regional",
    "Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the edge-optimized API endpoint",
    "Convert the API to a private API that only an interface endpoint can reach"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Every request is a unique write, so caching would not serve any responses from the cache.",
    "An edge-optimized endpoint routes requests through CloudFront edge locations, which adds a detour for callers in the same Region; a Regional endpoint serves them directly.",
    "Global Accelerator endpoints are load balancers, EC2 instances, and Elastic IP addresses, so it cannot front an API Gateway endpoint directly, and it would not remove the edge detour.",
    "A private API cannot be reached by the partners on the internet, which the company still needs."],
 ref:["d10e5","d10b2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q10-037", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A financial data provider streams market quotes to clients over a custom TCP-based protocol. The front end must handle millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency, pass the clients' source IP addresses through to the back-end servers, and expose one static IP address per Availability Zone that clients can add to their firewall allow lists. Which load balancing solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Application Load Balancer with cross-zone load balancing","Gateway Load Balancer with GENEVE encapsulation","Classic Load Balancer with TCP listeners","Network Load Balancer with an Elastic IP in each subnet"],
 a:[3],
 x:["An ALB works at layer 7 for HTTP, HTTPS, and gRPC, so it cannot balance a custom TCP protocol, and its IP addresses change as it scales.","Gateway Load Balancer inserts third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls into traffic flows; it is not a client-facing load balancer.","The Classic Load Balancer is a previous-generation service without static IP addresses or the NLB's performance.","An NLB operates at layer 4, scales to millions of requests per second with very low latency, preserves client source IPs, and can use an Elastic IP address in each AZ."],
 ref:["d10e5","d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q10-038", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Hybrid connectivity"],
 q:"A video post-production company moves several terabytes of footage every day between its data center and AWS. It currently uses a Site-to-Site VPN over the internet, and throughput varies widely by time of day, delaying deadlines. The company needs consistent, predictable bandwidth and latency for this ongoing transfer and can wait several weeks for the solution to be in place. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a second Site-to-Site VPN and use ECMP on a transit gateway","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for the footage uploads","Set up an AWS Direct Connect dedicated connection","Move the footage with AWS DataSync over the existing VPN"],
 a:[2],
 x:["ECMP across VPN tunnels can raise aggregate bandwidth, but the traffic still crosses the public internet, so performance stays inconsistent.","Transfer Acceleration improves long-distance transfers to S3 but still depends on the public internet between the data center and the edge, so it is not predictable.","Direct Connect provides a private, dedicated link between the data center and AWS with consistent bandwidth and latency; the weeks of lead time are acceptable here.","DataSync speeds up and automates transfers with parallel streams, but it would still run over the same internet VPN, so bandwidth and latency stay unpredictable."],
 ref:["d10e5","d2w1"], doc:"dx"},

{id:"q10-039", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Streaming","Analytics"],
 q:"A multi-tenant SaaS company streams JSON events from all of its customers through Amazon Data Firehose into one S3 bucket, where objects are written under prefixes based only on arrival time. Analysts query one customer at a time with Athena, and every query scans all customers' data. The company wants new data stored under customer_id and event-date prefixes as it arrives, without writing or running any transformation code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a new Firehose stream that uses dynamic partitioning with inline parsing of customer_id and date",
    "Add a Lambda transformation to the Firehose stream that rewrites each record and calls PutObject itself",
    "Run a nightly AWS Glue job that reads the new objects and rewrites them under customer and date prefixes",
    "Create a separate Firehose stream for each customer and route producers to the right stream in their code"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Dynamic partitioning extracts keys from each JSON record with inline parsing and writes the data to S3 prefixes built from those keys, so Athena can prune by customer and date, with no code to maintain. It can be turned on only when a stream is created, which is why a new stream replaces the old one.",
    "A Lambda function that writes objects itself is custom code, and Firehose transformation functions return records to Firehose rather than taking over its delivery.",
    "A nightly rewrite job is extra code and infrastructure, and the data is only partitioned the next day.",
    "One stream per customer multiplies the resources to manage and requires producer code changes for every new customer."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"], doc:"firehose"},

{id:"q10-040", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Hybrid connectivity","Global Accelerator"],
 q:"A retailer connects 40 stores on three continents to a transit gateway in eu-west-1 with AWS Site-to-Site VPN. Stores far from the Region suffer high latency and jitter because their traffic crosses long stretches of the public internet. The connections must stay IPsec-encrypted, and the company wants an improvement within days rather than waiting weeks for physical circuits. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Order AWS Direct Connect connections for every store","Replace each VPN with a VPC peering connection to the store","Put a CloudFront distribution in front of the transit gateway","Re-create the store VPNs as accelerated Site-to-Site VPN connections"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Direct Connect needs physical circuits at each location, which takes weeks or months and is costly for 40 stores.","VPC peering connects two VPCs; it cannot connect on-premises store networks.","CloudFront is a content delivery network for HTTP traffic; it cannot carry IPsec tunnels or front a transit gateway.","Accelerated Site-to-Site VPN uses AWS Global Accelerator to bring each tunnel onto the AWS global network at the nearest edge location, cutting latency and jitter while keeping IPsec; it works with transit gateway attachments and can be enabled only when a connection is created."],
 ref:["d10e5","d2w1","d10c1"]},

// ---- Task 3.5: data ingestion and transformation ----

{id:"q10-041", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A ride-hailing company ingests GPS updates into an Amazon Kinesis data stream in provisioned mode with 4 shards. During rush hour, producers receive <code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code> errors. Separately, five consumer applications that read the stream report growing lag because they share each shard's read throughput. Which combination of actions will resolve both problems? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Increase the stream's data retention period to 7 days","Add shards, or switch the stream to on-demand capacity mode","Replace the stream with an Amazon Data Firehose stream","Have each consumer call GetRecords more frequently","Register the consumers to use enhanced fan-out"],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Retention controls how long records are kept for replay; it does not add write or read throughput.","Each shard accepts up to 1 MB/s or 1,000 records per second of writes, so more shards (or on-demand mode) raise write capacity and stop the throttling.","A Firehose stream delivers to a single destination and cannot feed five independent custom consumer applications.","Shared-throughput consumers are limited to 2 MB/s and five GetRecords calls per second per shard in total, so polling harder only adds throttling.","Enhanced fan-out gives each registered consumer its own 2 MB/s per shard, pushed over HTTP/2, so the consumers no longer compete."],
 ref:["d10e6","d6c3","d10c3"], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q10-042", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Streaming","Analytics"],
 q:"A marketing analytics firm collects JSON clickstream events from its websites and needs them in S3 as compressed Parquet files, partitioned by date, for querying with Amazon Athena. A delay of a few minutes is acceptable. Each record must be lightly enriched by a small transformation function before delivery. The firm wants a fully managed solution with no consumer applications or servers to run. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Kinesis Data Streams with a consumer application on EC2 that writes Parquet to S3","Amazon Data Firehose with Lambda transformation and Parquet conversion to S3","Amazon MSK with Kafka Connect workers that write the events to S3","Amazon SQS with a Lambda consumer that writes each message to S3"],
 a:[1],
 x:["This works but requires building, running, and scaling a consumer application on servers, which the firm wants to avoid.","Firehose is fully managed, buffers records for near-real-time delivery, invokes Lambda to transform them, converts JSON to Parquet using a Glue table schema, and can partition the output in S3.","MSK requires running a Kafka cluster and connectors, far more to manage than simple delivery to S3 needs.","Writing one object per message creates millions of tiny JSON files with no Parquet conversion, which makes Athena queries slow."],
 ref:["d10e6","d6c3","d6w1"], doc:"firehose"},

{id:"q10-043", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A stock trading platform publishes trade events that three independent applications must process: fraud detection, a real-time dashboard, and an audit archiver. Each application must read every event, in order for a given account, within about a second of it being produced. The fraud team must also be able to reprocess the last 3 days of events after a model change. Which service should be used to ingest the events?",
 o:["Amazon Data Firehose with three destinations","An Amazon SQS FIFO queue grouped by account ID","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams partitioned by account ID","An Amazon SNS standard topic with three subscriptions"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A Firehose stream buffers and delivers to a single destination, and it does not let applications re-read past records.","Each message is processed and deleted by a single consumer, so three independent readers and replay of already-consumed events are not possible.","Kinesis Data Streams keeps per-shard ordering by partition key, lets multiple applications read the same records independently, and, with retention raised above the 24-hour default, lets consumers replay days of events.","SNS standard topics fan out messages but do not guarantee ordering and do not store messages for replay."],
 ref:["d10e6","d6c3"], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q10-044", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A logistics company is migrating an on-premises event platform built on Apache Kafka to AWS. Dozens of producer and consumer applications use the Kafka client libraries, Kafka Streams, and Kafka Connect. The company wants to move them with minimal code changes and to stop operating broker infrastructure itself, including patching and replacing failed brokers. Which service meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon MSK with MSK Connect for the existing connectors","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the Kinesis Client Library","Amazon MQ with a cluster of RabbitMQ brokers","Self-managed Kafka on EC2 in an Auto Scaling group"],
 a:[0],
 x:["MSK runs Apache Kafka as a managed service, so existing Kafka clients and Streams apps work with little change, MSK Connect runs the connectors, and AWS handles broker patching and replacement.","Kinesis Data Streams has its own API, so every producer and consumer would have to be rewritten.","Amazon MQ provides managed ActiveMQ and RabbitMQ brokers, which do not speak the Kafka protocol.","Self-managed Kafka on EC2 leaves the company operating, patching, and replacing brokers, which it wants to stop doing."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"]},

{id:"q10-045", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A healthcare analytics company receives new CSV and JSON datasets from partners in its S3 data lake every week, and schemas change occasionally as partners add columns. Analysts need to run ad hoc SQL queries on the data where it sits, without loading it into a database. The company wants table definitions discovered and kept current automatically, with no servers to manage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Load the files into Amazon Redshift with COPY commands and query there","Run Hive queries on an Amazon EMR cluster with a Hive metastore","Index the files in Amazon OpenSearch Service and query with its SQL plugin","Schedule AWS Glue crawlers to update the Data Catalog, and query with Athena"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Redshift COPY loads data into the warehouse, which the analysts want to avoid, and schema changes would need manual table updates.","EMR means running and managing a cluster, and the Hive table definitions would need to be maintained by hand.","OpenSearch requires ingesting and indexing the data into a domain first and is designed for search and log analytics, not ad hoc SQL over S3 files.","Glue crawlers infer schemas and update tables in the Data Catalog on a schedule, and Athena runs serverless SQL directly on the S3 data using those tables."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3","d6w1"], doc:"glue"},

{id:"q10-046", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Migration & transfer","S3 features"],
 q:"A healthcare clearinghouse receives claim files from 200 partner organizations. The partners use existing SFTP clients and scripts that cannot be changed. The files must land in Amazon S3, and a validation process must start automatically within seconds of each file's arrival. The company wants no servers to manage. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server that stores the uploaded files in the S3 bucket",
    "Run an SFTP server on an EC2 instance that mounts the bucket with Mountpoint for Amazon S3",
    "Deploy an AWS DataSync agent and run a task every hour that copies files from each partner",
    "Configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke a Lambda function that validates each new file",
    "Schedule a Lambda function every 15 minutes that lists the bucket and validates new files"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Transfer Family provides a fully managed SFTP endpoint backed by S3, so partners keep their clients and scripts and the files land directly in the bucket.",
    "A self-managed SFTP server on EC2 is a server to patch, scale, and keep available, which the company wants to avoid.",
    "DataSync would need an agent with access to every partner's storage and runs on a schedule; it is not an SFTP endpoint that partners can upload to.",
    "S3 Event Notifications invoke Lambda when each object is created, typically within seconds, so validation starts promptly without any servers.",
    "Polling every 15 minutes delays validation by up to 15 minutes and repeatedly lists the bucket, which misses the within-seconds requirement."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10e2"]},

{id:"q10-047", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A telecom company is moving an on-premises Hadoop environment to AWS. Its nightly pipelines use Apache Spark, Hive, and HBase with custom-compiled libraries pinned to specific framework versions, and engineers need to tune cluster settings and install software with bootstrap actions. The company also wants to cut costs by running part of the processing capacity on Spot Instances. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["AWS Glue ETL jobs using the Spark runtime","Amazon EMR with Spot Instances for task nodes","Amazon Athena with federated query connectors","Amazon Redshift with concurrency scaling enabled"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Glue runs serverless Spark jobs with limited control over the environment; it does not run HBase or Hive services or support bootstrap actions.","EMR runs Spark, Hive, HBase, and other Hadoop tools on clusters you configure, supports bootstrap actions and specific release versions, and can use Spot Instances for task nodes.","Athena runs serverless SQL queries; it is not a platform for existing Spark, Hive, and HBase pipelines with custom libraries.","Redshift is a data warehouse; concurrency scaling adds query capacity but does not run Hadoop ecosystem pipelines."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3","d6w1"], doc:"emr"},

{id:"q10-048", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Redshift","Analytics"],
 q:"A retail company keeps the last 13 months of sales data in an Amazon Redshift cluster and 10 years of older history as Parquet files in S3. Analysts occasionally need single queries that join the current Redshift tables with the historical data. The company does not want to load the history into the cluster or resize the cluster to hold it. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Load the history with the COPY command before each analysis","Enable concurrency scaling on the Redshift cluster","Query the S3 data through Redshift Spectrum external tables","Query the history with Athena and import the results into Redshift"],
 a:[2],
 x:["COPY loads data into cluster storage, which the company wants to avoid, and reloading for each analysis is slow.","Concurrency scaling adds temporary capacity for bursts of concurrent queries; it does not make S3 data queryable.","Redshift Spectrum queries data in S3 through external tables, so one SQL statement can join it with local tables without loading it or resizing the cluster.","This is a manual two-step process, and the imported results still consume cluster storage instead of being joined in place in one query."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3","d3w2"], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q10-049", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A financial services company has built an S3 data lake that analysts query with Athena, Redshift Spectrum, and Amazon EMR. Different teams must see only specific databases, tables, columns, and rows; for example, analysts must never see the account-number column. The company wants to manage these permissions centrally in one place instead of maintaining complex S3 bucket and IAM policies. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a separate S3 bucket per team holding only the columns it may see","Use AWS Lake Formation permissions with data filters on Data Catalog tables","Create an S3 access point per team with its own access point policy","Enable AWS Glue job bookmarks on the data lake's ETL jobs"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Copying data for every team multiplies storage and ETL work and creates copies that drift out of sync.","Lake Formation centrally grants database, table, column, row, and cell-level permissions that Athena, Redshift Spectrum, and EMR enforce.","Access points control access to buckets and prefixes, not to individual columns or rows inside the files.","Job bookmarks track which data an ETL job has already processed; they are not an access control feature."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"]},

{id:"q10-050", day:10, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A hotel chain wants interactive business dashboards for 2,000 regional managers. The data comes from Athena and Amazon Redshift, and dashboards must stay fast even on large datasets without querying the sources on every view. The company wants a fully managed, serverless BI service that scales to thousands of users with no servers to manage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon Managed Grafana workspaces","OpenSearch Dashboards on Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","Amazon Quick Sight dashboards backed by SPICE"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Managed Grafana targets operational observability of metrics, logs, and traces; it has no in-memory engine like SPICE for business datasets.","OpenSearch Dashboards visualizes data indexed in OpenSearch, mainly for search and log analytics, so the data would first have to be indexed there.","CloudWatch dashboards show operational metrics and logs for AWS resources, not business data from Athena and Redshift.","Amazon Quick Sight (formerly Amazon QuickSight) is a serverless BI service that scales to thousands of users, and its SPICE in-memory engine serves dashboards fast without hitting the sources on every view."],
 ref:["d10e6","d10c3"]}
);
